WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:02.510
- If the reason why you're
doing anything creative

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.622
is to make a living, than
you're doing it wrong.

00:00:04.622 --> 00:00:06.578
I don't really like the word inspiration.

00:00:06.578 --> 00:00:08.209
I like the word motivation.

00:00:08.209 --> 00:00:10.981
It's not how you tell
it, it's what you tell.

00:00:10.981 --> 00:00:14.790
Life shrinks and expands
in direct proportion

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.031
to one's willingness to take on risk.

00:00:17.031 --> 00:00:18.935
When you don't know the way,

00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:20.206
it's that you have to find the way.

00:00:20.206 --> 00:00:21.554
One of my most watched videos

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:24.431
it was shot terribly on a mobile phone.

00:00:24.431 --> 00:00:26.120
There will always be
someone who's better at

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.745
whatever you're doing than you are.

00:00:28.745 --> 00:00:31.142
I always do my best to
operate without fear.

00:00:31.142 --> 00:00:33.487
And the irony here is that
this video was like huge.

00:00:33.487 --> 00:00:34.976
This is before YouTube.

00:00:34.976 --> 00:00:39.236
This is when viral meant like
chlamydia in a frat house.

00:00:39.236 --> 00:00:41.338
You only get like a nugget of time

00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:43.692
to really pursue the
things you care about.

00:00:43.692 --> 00:00:46.945
- He's an American film
director, producer, designer

00:00:46.945 --> 00:00:49.034
and popular YouTube creator.

00:00:49.034 --> 00:00:49.867
- He's the co-founder of a

00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:51.942
social media company called Beme.

00:00:51.942 --> 00:00:53.382
- He and his brother are the founders

00:00:53.382 --> 00:00:56.389
of the HBO series, The Neistat Brothers.

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:57.932
- He's Casey Neistat and here are

00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:00.349
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:05.361
- I always made a living
so I could make movies.

00:01:05.361 --> 00:01:08.288
I never made movies to
try and make a living

00:01:08.288 --> 00:01:09.693
and I think that's a big mistake

00:01:09.693 --> 00:01:11.830
that a lot of newcomers
do is that they focus

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.247
on how can my passion pay me.

00:01:15.098 --> 00:01:17.506
And I think that's a
terrible place to start.

00:01:17.506 --> 00:01:19.416
If the reason why you're
doing anything creative

00:01:19.416 --> 00:01:22.094
is to make a living, then
you're doing it wrong.

00:01:22.094 --> 00:01:24.126
You get into it because
it's a true passion.

00:01:24.126 --> 00:01:26.489
It's something you really believe in.

00:01:26.489 --> 00:01:28.143
Or don't get into at all.

00:01:28.143 --> 00:01:29.964
What inspires me, you
know I don't really like

00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:31.623
the word inspiration.

00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:35.990
I like the motivation cause
I think the working part

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.000
always matters more than
the inspiration part.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.867
Work is always more
important than the idea.

00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:44.690
And as far as motivation...

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.448
You know I'm motivated
by everything around me.

00:01:47.448 --> 00:01:48.540
I'm motivated by my kid.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.315
I'm motivated by other people's work.

00:01:51.315 --> 00:01:54.380
I'm motivated by the
people that I work with.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.445
I'm like a sponge, I absorb
the motivation to do my work

00:01:57.445 --> 00:01:59.029
from everything around me.

00:01:59.029 --> 00:02:01.973
Well I think the key to vlogging well,

00:02:01.973 --> 00:02:05.671
and I think this is true
to writing to filmmaking

00:02:05.671 --> 00:02:07.701
to any sort of creative capacity.

00:02:07.701 --> 00:02:10.733
Any sort of field in
the creative capacity,

00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:14.313
is that it's not how you
tell it, it's what you tell.

00:02:14.313 --> 00:02:16.033
So it never has anything
to do with what camera

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:16.929
you shoot on--
- Yep, yep

00:02:16.929 --> 00:02:18.305
totally.
- It doesn't have to do

00:02:18.305 --> 00:02:21.638
with camera tricks or high
production or low production.

00:02:21.638 --> 00:02:22.885
It's what you're saying.

00:02:22.885 --> 00:02:24.140
That is the only thing that anyone

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.149
will ever respond to.

00:02:26.149 --> 00:02:28.494
So the advice I give to newcomers

00:02:28.494 --> 00:02:31.292
or to aspiring filmmakers or YouTubers

00:02:31.292 --> 00:02:33.829
is that shift all of your focus

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:36.054
from how to do it to what you're doing.

00:02:36.054 --> 00:02:37.841
What is it that you want to communicate?

00:02:37.841 --> 00:02:39.739
- Yeah.
- What drives you?

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:41.934
And if that's what your focus is,

00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:43.585
I think you'll be a much more effective

00:02:43.585 --> 00:02:44.577
sort of communicator

00:02:44.577 --> 00:02:45.721
and creator.
- Yeah.

00:02:45.721 --> 00:02:47.103
- Then if you're someone who obsesses over

00:02:47.103 --> 00:02:47.936
what lens they should be

00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:49.170
shooting on.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.517
- When I look back at my career,
I see all of these tangents

00:02:52.517 --> 00:02:55.299
and the tangents that have
always yielded success

00:02:55.299 --> 00:02:58.145
were the ones where there
was no established path.

00:02:58.145 --> 00:03:00.215
No defined route for me to take.

00:03:00.215 --> 00:03:01.817
But I went down it anyways.

00:03:01.817 --> 00:03:03.570
And through that kind of exploration,

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.640
I always discovered something new.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.251
And those new things, those new entities,

00:03:08.251 --> 00:03:10.348
whether they be movies or ideas

00:03:10.348 --> 00:03:11.986
or things in my personal life,

00:03:11.986 --> 00:03:14.947
have always proven to be
the most rewarding for me.

00:03:14.947 --> 00:03:18.720
Life shrinks and expands
in direct proportion

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.338
to one's willingness to take on risks.

00:03:21.338 --> 00:03:23.490
And I think when it comes to exploring

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.161
the act of exploration is
the act of assuming risk.

00:03:27.161 --> 00:03:29.864
The greater risk takers
the greater explorer.

00:03:29.864 --> 00:03:31.098
I never went to film school.

00:03:31.098 --> 00:03:33.408
I never sort of apprenticed
under a filmmaker.

00:03:33.408 --> 00:03:34.289
I was never taught.

00:03:34.289 --> 00:03:35.122
I never had a mentor.

00:03:35.122 --> 00:03:37.077
I never had anybody that I followed.

00:03:37.077 --> 00:03:39.002
And the interesting thing that happens

00:03:39.002 --> 00:03:41.022
when you don't know the way

00:03:41.022 --> 00:03:42.810
is that you have to find the way.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.179
And I always get to the destination,

00:03:45.179 --> 00:03:46.709
which are these finished movies.

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:49.747
But the path with which I employ

00:03:49.747 --> 00:03:52.731
is radically different
from what's the norm.

00:03:52.731 --> 00:03:54.322
And again, that's not some...

00:03:54.322 --> 00:03:58.587
I don't take credit for that
as some genius stylistic move.

00:03:58.587 --> 00:04:00.356
It's just I don't know, like...

00:04:00.356 --> 00:04:03.216
I still don't know how to
do proper titles, titling.

00:04:03.216 --> 00:04:05.390
Like my assistant editor did those titles

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.218
which is why most of the
movies on my YouTube channel

00:04:07.218 --> 00:04:09.578
all the titles are just
handwritten on paper

00:04:09.578 --> 00:04:12.089
and then the piece of paper's filmed.

00:04:12.089 --> 00:04:13.562
Cause I don't know how to do it right.

00:04:13.562 --> 00:04:15.449
I don't know how to do After Effects.

00:04:15.449 --> 00:04:19.247
So all my animation is like stop frame.

00:04:19.247 --> 00:04:21.992
And now sort of in success,
I have the opportunities

00:04:21.992 --> 00:04:26.439
to use all of the tools that
were always so elusive to me.

00:04:26.439 --> 00:04:28.582
So now it's a much more conscious decision

00:04:28.582 --> 00:04:31.342
to maintain this sort
of handmade aesthetic.

00:04:31.342 --> 00:04:35.509
I think now that so much
media is consumed on mobile...

00:04:37.564 --> 00:04:40.197
The cinematography, the cinematic aspects

00:04:40.197 --> 00:04:41.884
are being marginalized.

00:04:41.884 --> 00:04:43.484
And what's taking it's place is sort of

00:04:43.484 --> 00:04:45.244
a more relatable story.

00:04:45.244 --> 00:04:47.997
I think people, one of
my most watched videos

00:04:47.997 --> 00:04:50.495
was shot terribly on a mobile phone.

00:04:50.495 --> 00:04:54.239
But the story is so poignant
that 13 or 14 million people

00:04:54.239 --> 00:04:56.483
still elected to see it.

00:04:56.483 --> 00:04:58.495
So I think there will always be a place

00:04:58.495 --> 00:05:01.188
and an appreciation
for really high quality

00:05:01.188 --> 00:05:03.672
cinematic production appeal.

00:05:03.672 --> 00:05:06.627
But there's a new place
that's being presented

00:05:06.627 --> 00:05:10.065
that mobile has presenting
that we'll forgive

00:05:10.065 --> 00:05:13.241
short comings in production
provided that the story's there.

00:05:13.241 --> 00:05:15.889
Provided the content is
something people care about.

00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:18.723
There are two rules
that I always adhere to

00:05:18.723 --> 00:05:22.234
and that is to work hard and be brave.

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:24.544
And I think the essence of hard work

00:05:24.544 --> 00:05:26.576
is one that's pretty
straight forward is that

00:05:26.576 --> 00:05:28.301
you'll never be the best looking,

00:05:28.301 --> 00:05:30.809
you'll never be the tallest,
most talented, most capable.

00:05:30.809 --> 00:05:32.400
You'll never have the most money.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.976
There will always be someone who's better

00:05:33.976 --> 00:05:36.962
at whatever you're doing than you are.

00:05:36.962 --> 00:05:38.607
But you can always be the hardest working

00:05:38.607 --> 00:05:40.151
person in the room.

00:05:40.151 --> 00:05:43.111
And I think the hardest
working person will always win.

00:05:43.111 --> 00:05:44.864
Right after the HBO show aired

00:05:44.864 --> 00:05:47.357
I produced two feature
films that were big hits.

00:05:47.357 --> 00:05:50.254
They premiered at like Cannes
Film Festival and Sundance and

00:05:50.254 --> 00:05:52.100
I like got an award that
Natalie Portman gave me

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.830
on TV that my dad watched.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.071
And it was like a big, big deal.

00:05:56.071 --> 00:05:58.079
And I just like, I
remember having this moment

00:05:58.079 --> 00:06:00.941
where I realized that like I
was so deep into this thing

00:06:00.941 --> 00:06:03.638
that wasn't really, it
didn't feel right to me.

00:06:03.638 --> 00:06:06.566
And it's different trying
to find opportunities

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.174
when you are in a place of success,

00:06:08.174 --> 00:06:09.837
when you're in a place of comfort.

00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:11.043
Like I was no longer starving.

00:06:11.043 --> 00:06:12.089
I wasn't hungry anymore.

00:06:12.089 --> 00:06:14.179
I had some recognition.

00:06:14.179 --> 00:06:15.531
But something didn't feel right

00:06:15.531 --> 00:06:17.795
and that was when I took this big pivot

00:06:17.795 --> 00:06:19.984
and that pivot was to like, you know what

00:06:19.984 --> 00:06:21.546
it's not the politics.

00:06:21.546 --> 00:06:23.454
It's not the Natalie
Portman giving me the award.

00:06:23.454 --> 00:06:26.365
It's not the schmoozing that I love.

00:06:26.365 --> 00:06:30.039
I love telling stories,
telling stories is my passion.

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:33.069
And that was when I decided
to sort of walk away from TV

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:34.746
and walk away from movies.

00:06:34.746 --> 00:06:37.133
And I just wanted to make YouTube videos.

00:06:37.133 --> 00:06:40.757
And I remember like my big Hollywood agent

00:06:40.757 --> 00:06:43.196
when I sort of told
that to him he was like,

00:06:43.196 --> 00:06:44.779
"Oh God, Casey no."

00:06:45.865 --> 00:06:46.790
You know, this was four years ago.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:47.998
YouTube, five years ago now.

00:06:47.998 --> 00:06:51.162
YouTube was nothing, it was
something that was silly.

00:06:51.162 --> 00:06:52.421
It was like a really great place to go

00:06:52.421 --> 00:06:54.506
and watch cats play the piano.

00:06:54.506 --> 00:06:57.886
And I wanted to like parlay my career

00:06:57.886 --> 00:07:01.303
as a very successful filmmaker into that.

00:07:02.322 --> 00:07:03.930
And I can't tell you the opportunities

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.263
that were birthed from that.

00:07:08.233 --> 00:07:10.682
It's given me what I have today.

00:07:10.682 --> 00:07:11.954
That's opportunity.

00:07:11.954 --> 00:07:13.521
Those are opportunities.

00:07:13.521 --> 00:07:14.816
Those are opportunities that in the moment

00:07:14.816 --> 00:07:16.122
I couldn't have told
you were opportunities.

00:07:16.122 --> 00:07:17.402
I didn't know what they were.

00:07:17.402 --> 00:07:19.098
There was something that just felt right.

00:07:19.098 --> 00:07:20.767
There was something that looked right.

00:07:20.767 --> 00:07:22.184
I did my best calculations.

00:07:22.184 --> 00:07:23.668
I took my best guess.

00:07:23.668 --> 00:07:25.629
It wasn't reckless.

00:07:25.629 --> 00:07:27.981
I never operate recklessly
and this is an important

00:07:27.981 --> 00:07:32.688
differentiation between
recklessness and fearlessness.

00:07:32.688 --> 00:07:35.085
I always do my best to
operate without fear.

00:07:35.085 --> 00:07:37.083
Don't not do something.

00:07:37.083 --> 00:07:39.387
Don't skip something
cause I'm afraid to do it.

00:07:39.387 --> 00:07:42.509
But make sure it's a smart decision.

00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:45.842
And then part two of that is obligation.

00:07:47.142 --> 00:07:51.247
I feel obliged to embrace
opportunities like that.

00:07:51.247 --> 00:07:52.871
I felt obliged to quit my job.

00:07:52.871 --> 00:07:55.038
I felt obliged to walk away from HBO.

00:07:55.038 --> 00:07:58.172
And the obligation was because
there was something in me.

00:07:58.172 --> 00:08:00.844
There was a truth that I wanted.

00:08:00.844 --> 00:08:03.186
And the obligation was to embrace that.

00:08:03.186 --> 00:08:07.353
The first movie we made that
anybody saw was in 2003.

00:08:09.283 --> 00:08:11.306
And this is a good
story for Eddie Lampour.

00:08:11.306 --> 00:08:13.745
The talk yesterday about
biting the hand that feeds you.

00:08:13.745 --> 00:08:17.918
So in 2002 I think, Apple
came out with the iPod.

00:08:17.918 --> 00:08:21.125
And in 2003, my iPod battery died.

00:08:21.125 --> 00:08:23.022
And I called up, I was like
dead broke at the time.

00:08:23.022 --> 00:08:24.683
It was a present and I
called up Apple to fix it

00:08:24.683 --> 00:08:27.397
and they were like, "Just buy a new one."

00:08:27.397 --> 00:08:28.838
And it really pissed me off.

00:08:28.838 --> 00:08:31.876
So I called them back but
I recorded the phone call.

00:08:31.876 --> 00:08:32.709
And...

00:08:33.725 --> 00:08:35.509
And this is at the time, I know.

00:08:35.509 --> 00:08:39.009
This was at the time when
Apple had those like,

00:08:39.009 --> 00:08:42.698
awesome like, those silhouette
with the colors posters

00:08:42.698 --> 00:08:43.800
all over New York City.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.512
I mean the whole city was covered in them.

00:08:45.512 --> 00:08:49.217
So I recorded the phone call
and then my brother Van and I

00:08:49.217 --> 00:08:51.264
went around with this stencil

00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:53.592
and we spray painted,
"iPods unreplaceable battery

00:08:53.592 --> 00:08:55.208
"lasts only 18 months."

00:08:55.208 --> 00:08:57.529
On every one of these in New York City.

00:08:57.529 --> 00:09:00.362
(audience laughs)

00:09:01.994 --> 00:09:04.467
And we didn't think, I mean
sure we were wise asses

00:09:04.467 --> 00:09:06.220
but we just thought
we'd educate the masses.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:09.685
Like if Apple wasn't going to
do it, we thought we would.

00:09:09.685 --> 00:09:12.020
And the irony here is that
this video was like huge.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:13.453
This is before YouTube.

00:09:13.453 --> 00:09:15.559
This is when viral meant like

00:09:15.559 --> 00:09:18.929
chlamydia in a frat house, like.

00:09:18.929 --> 00:09:20.520
This thing was huge.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:22.764
Like this was emailed all over the place.

00:09:22.764 --> 00:09:24.264
Millions of views.

00:09:25.242 --> 00:09:27.787
Steve Jobs sending me hate mail.

00:09:27.787 --> 00:09:29.953
And then like a week later,
Apple changed the policy

00:09:29.953 --> 00:09:32.498
and they were like, "Yeah we
were going to do it anyways."

00:09:32.498 --> 00:09:35.952
(audience laughs)

00:09:35.952 --> 00:09:38.946
Which is great but again, this video

00:09:38.946 --> 00:09:41.978
brought a lot of eyeballs
to my brother and me.

00:09:41.978 --> 00:09:43.874
And people got to see all these
other little stupid videos

00:09:43.874 --> 00:09:45.572
that we'd been making for years.

00:09:45.572 --> 00:09:47.128
And I tried to like find
some press for you guys.

00:09:47.128 --> 00:09:49.395
This is seven years ago,
so I couldn't really find.

00:09:49.395 --> 00:09:50.882
But this is some of that.

00:09:50.882 --> 00:09:52.608
And anyhow, it brought a
lot of attention to us.

00:09:52.608 --> 00:09:54.453
And if anything, it drove
home the point that like

00:09:54.453 --> 00:09:57.244
you don't need any of that
understood infrastructure.

00:09:57.244 --> 00:09:58.738
This is just like us being pissed off

00:09:58.738 --> 00:09:59.893
and that was enough motivation.

00:09:59.893 --> 00:10:00.961
So we went and made this movie

00:10:00.961 --> 00:10:02.697
and now we got all this attention.

00:10:02.697 --> 00:10:05.221
And we drove that home
for, stuck with that

00:10:05.221 --> 00:10:08.305
for a really long time and now

00:10:08.305 --> 00:10:11.160
you know, I make sort of serious movies.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.144
I just a shot a documentary,
a feature length doc

00:10:13.144 --> 00:10:15.794
in Afghanistan which is
going to be kind of serious.

00:10:15.794 --> 00:10:16.874
But I never let go of that

00:10:16.874 --> 00:10:21.042
and I still make sort of
like wise-ass goth movies.

00:10:21.042 --> 00:10:23.451
And this summer, this is a good one.

00:10:23.451 --> 00:10:24.426
I just want to tell this story.

00:10:24.426 --> 00:10:27.006
But this summer, I was riding
my bike through New York City

00:10:27.006 --> 00:10:29.670
and I was pulled over in the pouring rain

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.073
by a police officer for riding my bike

00:10:32.073 --> 00:10:34.286
outside the bike lane.

00:10:34.286 --> 00:10:36.828
Which, come on man, that's
like give me a break.

00:10:36.828 --> 00:10:39.824
So I filmed the cop on
my iPhone naturally.

00:10:39.824 --> 00:10:43.643
And then I made a movie of me going around

00:10:43.643 --> 00:10:45.412
trying to show the officer

00:10:45.412 --> 00:10:47.364
why you can't always
ride in the bike lanes.

00:10:47.364 --> 00:10:50.022
And I crashed into everything I found.

00:10:50.022 --> 00:10:52.037
So the video's just me arguing with a cop

00:10:52.037 --> 00:10:53.681
for like 30 seconds
then me crashing my bike

00:10:53.681 --> 00:10:55.574
for another two minutes.

00:10:55.574 --> 00:10:57.135
And this thing was huge.

00:10:57.135 --> 00:10:58.986
I mean this thing was
bigger than the iPod movie.

00:10:58.986 --> 00:11:02.273
This was gigantic like, it
was on every news outlet

00:11:02.273 --> 00:11:06.516
and Bloomberg had to answer
to it in a press conference.

00:11:06.516 --> 00:11:09.382
And what is this, The
Guardian called me a warrior

00:11:09.382 --> 00:11:10.589
which is awesome.

00:11:10.589 --> 00:11:11.422
And...

00:11:13.109 --> 00:11:15.575
And it was huge and...

00:11:15.575 --> 00:11:17.719
This again is another good
exemplification of the idea

00:11:17.719 --> 00:11:19.456
that it's just embrace the
resources that you have

00:11:19.456 --> 00:11:20.751
available to you.

00:11:20.751 --> 00:11:22.294
And this is what I try to
talk to young kids about

00:11:22.294 --> 00:11:23.448
and this is what I try to reinforce

00:11:23.448 --> 00:11:25.214
is that like let go of all the pretentions

00:11:25.214 --> 00:11:26.869
that is filmmaking and embrace

00:11:26.869 --> 00:11:28.582
what you have available to you.

00:11:28.582 --> 00:11:30.448
I want to talk about risk.

00:11:30.448 --> 00:11:34.671
As a guiding principle, life
shrinks and life expands

00:11:34.671 --> 00:11:38.578
in direct proportion to your
willingness to assume risk.

00:11:38.578 --> 00:11:41.115
Yeah, like I've made some
really stupid decisions

00:11:41.115 --> 00:11:43.708
in my career for my entire career.

00:11:43.708 --> 00:11:45.861
On a broad plane, they've all worked out.

00:11:45.861 --> 00:11:47.930
Every time I've quit my jobs,

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:49.869
which I've done every time I've had a job,

00:11:49.869 --> 00:11:52.492
people that I trust most
that treat people that I love

00:11:52.492 --> 00:11:54.002
all advised against it.

00:11:54.002 --> 00:11:56.875
My dad told me not to
move to New York City

00:11:56.875 --> 00:11:58.634
because it was such a risk.

00:11:58.634 --> 00:12:01.719
And every time I took this bigger risks,

00:12:01.719 --> 00:12:04.615
the opportunity for a larger
payout was always there.

00:12:04.615 --> 00:12:09.148
Life is like this super
temporary, mega fragile thing.

00:12:09.148 --> 00:12:11.220
You only get like a nugget of time

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:13.425
to really pursue the
things you care about.

00:12:13.425 --> 00:12:14.925
And like I'm 30...

00:12:16.144 --> 00:12:20.335
And my rule is that the right
time is always right now.

00:12:20.335 --> 00:12:21.862
To put a little meat on these bones,

00:12:21.862 --> 00:12:25.388
this new company that I
don't really talk about...

00:12:25.388 --> 00:12:29.536
This new company is the
biggest risk I've ever taken.

00:12:29.536 --> 00:12:34.369
I took five years of my life
to build my advertising career

00:12:34.369 --> 00:12:36.209
and I made like a lot of money.

00:12:36.209 --> 00:12:37.914
I've been doing really great.

00:12:37.914 --> 00:12:39.034
Look at my YouTube channel.

00:12:39.034 --> 00:12:40.075
Go all the way back.

00:12:40.075 --> 00:12:41.650
The last branded content thing you'll see

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.267
was in February of 2014.

00:12:44.267 --> 00:12:47.656
I ditched it all to pursue
something I knew nothing about.

00:12:47.656 --> 00:12:50.026
This huge risk and I did it at a time

00:12:50.026 --> 00:12:51.973
when my wife was pregnant

00:12:51.973 --> 00:12:53.675
and there was all of this vulnerability

00:12:53.675 --> 00:12:55.532
and all this scariness in my life.

00:12:55.532 --> 00:12:59.308
And even now at 33, my dad said, 34.

00:12:59.308 --> 00:13:01.237
My dad said, "Casey that's crazy.

00:13:01.237 --> 00:13:03.280
"You've got a good
thing, don't let it go."

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.256
But I know that like someday,
I won't be able to do this.

00:13:07.256 --> 00:13:09.567
I know that the time is now.

00:13:09.567 --> 00:13:11.424
The right time is always now.

00:13:11.424 --> 00:13:12.577
So I had to do it.

00:13:12.577 --> 00:13:15.023
That's how I feel about risk in general.

00:13:15.023 --> 00:13:17.176
And that's what the struggle
has always been for me

00:13:17.176 --> 00:13:19.792
is like identifying risk, identifying fear

00:13:19.792 --> 00:13:21.664
and then just smashing through it.

00:13:21.664 --> 00:13:25.014
Because a fear is looking back and wishing

00:13:25.014 --> 00:13:27.208
that I had done these things.

00:13:27.208 --> 00:13:30.476
The most dangerous thing
you can do in your career,

00:13:30.476 --> 00:13:34.643
the most dangerous thing you
can do in life is play it safe.

00:13:37.268 --> 00:13:38.492
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:38.492 --> 00:13:39.491
I hope you enjoyed.

00:13:39.491 --> 00:13:42.005
I'd love to know which
of Casey's top ten rules

00:13:42.005 --> 00:13:44.019
had the biggest impact on you.

00:13:44.019 --> 00:13:44.999
Leave it in the comments below

00:13:44.999 --> 00:13:46.370
and we're going to join the discussion.

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:47.749
- And we'd also love to know

00:13:47.749 --> 00:13:48.941
who are your favorite entrepreneurs

00:13:48.941 --> 00:13:51.350
that you want us to
feature on this channel?

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:52.628
Leave that in the comments below.

00:13:52.628 --> 00:13:53.812
- Thank you guys for watching.

00:13:53.812 --> 00:13:55.284
Continue to believe.

00:13:55.284 --> 00:13:57.951
- [Both] And we'll see you soon.

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.748
- I don't think in term of like

00:14:05.748 --> 00:14:08.268
retrospectively, retroactively
looking at what I did

00:14:08.268 --> 00:14:09.725
and how I got to where I am.

00:14:09.725 --> 00:14:11.918
Instead, it's much more of a focus on

00:14:11.918 --> 00:14:13.579
what do I want to do next?

00:14:13.579 --> 00:14:15.047
Because I think that
if you try to consider

00:14:15.047 --> 00:14:17.608
or cater to your audience too much,

00:14:17.608 --> 00:14:21.237
you'll invariably end up
making something that's mush.

00:14:21.237 --> 00:14:22.604
- Yeah.
- Because I don't know

00:14:22.604 --> 00:14:24.908
that the audience knows
what they want to see.

00:14:24.908 --> 00:14:27.288
I know that I don't know what's
going to peak my interests.

00:14:27.288 --> 00:14:29.646
If I knew how to describe
the perfect movie,

00:14:29.646 --> 00:14:32.154
vlog, television show, I would just do it.

00:14:32.154 --> 00:14:34.575
I want to see something that surprises me.

00:14:34.575 --> 00:14:36.999
And when I think of my own
work, that's exactly right.

00:14:36.999 --> 00:14:40.664
Like I put my head down and
I try to ignore the world.

00:14:40.664 --> 00:14:42.899
And I try to make something
that I think is really good.

00:14:42.899 --> 00:14:44.156
- Yeah, yeah.
- And if people like it,

00:14:44.156 --> 00:14:44.989
great.

00:14:44.989 --> 00:14:46.305
If they don't, I don't care.

00:14:46.305 --> 00:14:48.713
As long as I like it.

00:14:48.713 --> 00:14:51.457
So I think like if I had
one thing to attribute

00:14:51.457 --> 00:14:53.659
all of this growth to,
it's that I'm making work

00:14:53.659 --> 00:14:56.802
that I feel embodies the
same level of integrity

00:14:56.802 --> 00:14:59.694
of anything else that I
would invest myself into.

00:14:59.694 --> 00:15:02.062
My advice to young people
that want to be filmmakers

00:15:02.062 --> 00:15:03.534
and want to do anything
in the creative world

00:15:03.534 --> 00:15:06.723
is always the same and that's do the work.

00:15:06.723 --> 00:15:08.349
Ideas are cheap.

00:15:08.349 --> 00:15:10.472
I think inspiration is cheap.

00:15:10.472 --> 00:15:11.692
I discount all of those.

00:15:11.692 --> 00:15:13.724
Those mean very little to me.

00:15:13.724 --> 00:15:15.596
All I care about is the work

00:15:15.596 --> 00:15:17.405
because when I started filmmaking,

00:15:17.405 --> 00:15:18.557
I wasn't much of a filmmaker.

00:15:18.557 --> 00:15:20.676
I had a really junky
point and shoot camera

00:15:20.676 --> 00:15:22.754
and I had iMovie.

00:15:22.754 --> 00:15:24.961
I've never learned the
technicalities of filmmaking.

00:15:24.961 --> 00:15:29.128
It's only through doing,
that I learned how to create.

00:15:31.889 --> 00:15:34.972
Well you know, I think fulfillment...

00:15:36.997 --> 00:15:37.934
Fulfillment and YouTube producing

00:15:37.934 --> 00:15:38.767
are kind of two different things.

00:15:38.767 --> 00:15:41.612
I think fulfillment in
general as a filmmaker for me

00:15:41.612 --> 00:15:43.230
comes from creating.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:45.773
And that's why you know in 2010,

00:15:45.773 --> 00:15:47.172
they put the soundtrack on for the story

00:15:47.172 --> 00:15:50.284
so I'll try to make it sound nostalgic.

00:15:50.284 --> 00:15:53.406
I left like the mainstream
sort of universe

00:15:53.406 --> 00:15:55.508
cause I wanted to create more.

00:15:55.508 --> 00:15:58.359
So for me, fulfillment comes in creation.

00:15:58.359 --> 00:16:01.053
And I always say that like
I'm a little bit of a junky

00:16:01.053 --> 00:16:03.421
and my drug is uploading.

00:16:03.421 --> 00:16:06.212
Because if I haven't posted
a movie in like a month

00:16:06.212 --> 00:16:09.213
or a month and a half, I get
like seriously depressed.

00:16:09.213 --> 00:16:10.692
And the only that like makes me happy

00:16:10.692 --> 00:16:13.757
is to get a new movie out there.

00:16:13.757 --> 00:16:15.513
That answers your question, right?

